WEBVTT

00:00:00.341 --> 00:00:02.971
Last time, ball B left the bench sideways,

00:00:03.292 --> 00:00:05.661
so it started with no vertical velocity at

00:00:05.741 --> 00:00:05.841
all.

00:00:06.605 --> 00:00:08.151
Now let's tilt the launcher up.

00:00:08.815 --> 00:00:10.662
This one fires a ball at twenty metres

00:00:10.683 --> 00:00:13.474
per second, at thirty degrees above the horizontal.

00:00:14.258 --> 00:00:16.367
Some of that velocity carries the ball across,

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:17.974
and some carries it up.

00:00:18.639 --> 00:00:19.845
But how much of each?

00:00:20.751 --> 00:00:22.580
Thirty degrees is a third of the way

00:00:22.641 --> 00:00:24.008
from horizontal to vertical.

00:00:24.512 --> 00:00:25.865
So does a third of the speed go

00:00:25.926 --> 00:00:26.026
up?

00:00:26.737 --> 00:00:27.082
Have a think.

00:00:31.321 --> 00:00:33.724
The launch velocity is a vector, so we

00:00:33.744 --> 00:00:35.687
can split it into two components at right

00:00:35.747 --> 00:00:38.410
angles: a horizontal one, u x, and a

00:00:38.471 --> 00:00:39.752
vertical one, u y.

00:00:40.594 --> 00:00:42.055
Put them tip to tail and they make

00:00:42.116 --> 00:00:44.841
a right-angled triangle, with the launch velocity as

00:00:44.882 --> 00:00:45.502
the hypotenuse.

00:00:46.305 --> 00:00:48.951
The angle theta sits between the launch velocity

00:00:49.153 --> 00:00:49.874
and the horizontal.

00:00:50.618 --> 00:00:52.684
So the horizontal side is next to the

00:00:52.765 --> 00:00:54.189
angle, the adjacent side,

00:00:54.692 --> 00:00:56.236
and the vertical side is opposite it.

00:00:56.859 --> 00:01:00.308
From right-angled triangles, adjacent over hypotenuse is cos

00:01:00.349 --> 00:01:03.357
theta, and opposite over hypotenuse is sin theta.

00:01:03.939 --> 00:01:06.584
So u x equals u cos theta, and

00:01:06.705 --> 00:01:08.869
u y equals u sin theta.

00:01:09.649 --> 00:01:11.299
Let's do the twenty metres per second at

00:01:11.381 --> 00:01:12.004
thirty degrees.

00:01:12.809 --> 00:01:15.819
Horizontally, u x is twenty times cos thirty,

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:18.207
which is about seventeen point three metres per

00:01:18.228 --> 00:01:18.468
second.

00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:22.129
Vertically, u y is twenty times sin thirty.

00:01:22.854 --> 00:01:24.997
Sin thirty is exactly a half, so u

00:01:25.219 --> 00:01:27.202
y is ten point zero metres per second,

00:01:27.624 --> 00:01:27.904
upwards.

00:01:28.709 --> 00:01:30.520
So half the speed goes up, not a

00:01:30.561 --> 00:01:30.782
third.

00:01:31.507 --> 00:01:34.616
And notice, seventeen point three plus ten isn't

00:01:34.677 --> 00:01:34.957
twenty.

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:37.692
The components aren't pieces of a total that

00:01:37.733 --> 00:01:38.617
you add as numbers.

00:01:39.061 --> 00:01:41.026
They're at right angles, so they combine by

00:01:41.068 --> 00:01:41.669
Pythagoras,

00:01:42.171 --> 00:01:43.856
and the square root of seventeen point three

00:01:43.897 --> 00:01:46.063
squared plus ten squared is twenty.

00:01:46.628 --> 00:01:47.934
Now watch what the angle does.

00:01:48.658 --> 00:01:51.223
At zero degrees, like ball B last time,

00:01:51.745 --> 00:01:54.329
all twenty metres per second goes across, and

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:55.171
none goes up.

00:01:55.934 --> 00:01:58.626
As the launcher tilts up, u y grows

00:01:58.929 --> 00:02:00.274
and u x shrinks.

00:02:00.998 --> 00:02:03.945
At forty-five degrees they're equal, about fourteen point

00:02:04.046 --> 00:02:04.366
one each.

00:02:05.069 --> 00:02:07.841
Keep going, and at ninety degrees it's all

00:02:07.902 --> 00:02:09.468
vertical, with nothing across.

00:02:10.213 --> 00:02:11.842
The launch speed never changes.

00:02:12.265 --> 00:02:14.455
The angle only decides how that speed is

00:02:14.557 --> 00:02:16.184
shared between the two directions.

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:18.737
Once the ball's in the air, the two

00:02:18.778 --> 00:02:20.506
components behave just as before.

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:23.395
u x never changes, because there's still no

00:02:23.477 --> 00:02:24.378
horizontal force.

00:02:24.981 --> 00:02:27.888
But u y now points up, while gravity

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:28.650
points down.

00:02:29.353 --> 00:02:32.457
Taking up as positive, a y is minus

00:02:32.578 --> 00:02:35.302
nine point eight, so the vertical velocity drops

00:02:35.403 --> 00:02:37.746
by nine point eight metres per second every

00:02:37.787 --> 00:02:38.086
second.

00:02:38.708 --> 00:02:41.232
Starting at ten, after one second it's down

00:02:41.273 --> 00:02:42.575
to just zero point two,

00:02:43.257 --> 00:02:44.739
and a moment later it runs out.

00:02:45.264 --> 00:02:46.215
That's the highest point.

00:02:47.109 --> 00:02:49.118
So what's happening right at the top?

00:02:49.721 --> 00:02:52.223
What's the ball's velocity there, and what's its

00:02:52.284 --> 00:02:52.964
acceleration?

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:54.032
Have a think.

00:02:57.769 --> 00:03:00.141
At the top, the vertical velocity is zero

00:03:00.182 --> 00:03:00.784
for an instant.

00:03:01.248 --> 00:03:03.134
But the ball is still moving, because its

00:03:03.195 --> 00:03:05.964
horizontal velocity is still seventeen point three metres

00:03:05.985 --> 00:03:06.466
per second.

00:03:07.009 --> 00:03:09.166
So its velocity at the peak isn't zero.

00:03:09.490 --> 00:03:10.154
It's horizontal.

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:12.895
And the acceleration hasn't changed at all.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:15.528
Gravity never switches off, so it's still nine

00:03:15.569 --> 00:03:17.758
point eight metres per second squared, downwards.

00:03:18.281 --> 00:03:19.607
That's what turns the ball around.

00:03:20.211 --> 00:03:22.176
If the acceleration were zero at the top,

00:03:22.538 --> 00:03:24.883
the vertical velocity would stay at zero, and

00:03:24.904 --> 00:03:26.688
the ball would just keep going sideways at

00:03:26.729 --> 00:03:27.129
that height.

00:03:27.833 --> 00:03:30.690
Zero vertical velocity isn't zero acceleration.

00:03:34.376 --> 00:03:35.201
Here's how to use that.

00:03:35.805 --> 00:03:37.793
A ball is launched at twenty-five metres per

00:03:37.835 --> 00:03:40.084
second, at forty degrees above the horizontal.

00:03:40.547 --> 00:03:41.711
How long does it take to reach its

00:03:41.792 --> 00:03:42.454
highest point?

00:03:43.218 --> 00:03:45.204
Only the vertical column can tell us, so

00:03:45.286 --> 00:03:45.766
start there.

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:48.736
Up is positive, so a y is minus

00:03:48.777 --> 00:03:49.357
nine point eight.

00:03:50.121 --> 00:03:53.033
u y is twenty-five sin forty, about sixteen

00:03:53.074 --> 00:03:54.239
point one metres per second.

00:03:54.984 --> 00:03:56.859
At the peak, v y is zero.

00:03:57.503 --> 00:03:59.926
So using v equals u plus a t,

00:04:00.467 --> 00:04:03.811
zero equals sixteen point zero seven minus nine

00:04:03.872 --> 00:04:04.672
point eight t,

00:04:05.194 --> 00:04:06.975
and t is about one point six four

00:04:07.016 --> 00:04:07.336
seconds.

00:04:07.778 --> 00:04:09.403
That's the time to the top, not the

00:04:09.425 --> 00:04:09.865
whole flight.

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:11.408
Two traps here.

00:04:11.936 --> 00:04:13.906
The first is putting the whole twenty-five metres

00:04:13.947 --> 00:04:15.314
per second into that equation.

00:04:15.858 --> 00:04:17.645
That gives two point five five seconds, which

00:04:17.706 --> 00:04:19.996
is wrong, because only the vertical component is

00:04:20.037 --> 00:04:20.638
fighting gravity.

00:04:21.363 --> 00:04:22.997
The second is an angle measured from the

00:04:23.079 --> 00:04:23.501
vertical.

00:04:24.126 --> 00:04:26.377
Forty degrees from the vertical is fifty degrees

00:04:26.519 --> 00:04:27.342
above the horizontal.

00:04:27.966 --> 00:04:29.670
Now the vertical side is the one next

00:04:29.691 --> 00:04:31.996
to the angle, so u y is twenty-five

00:04:32.077 --> 00:04:32.798
cos forty.

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:35.366
Always check which side the angle is next

00:04:35.407 --> 00:04:37.192
to before you choose sin or cos.

00:04:37.882 --> 00:04:38.869
Here's a way to remember it:

00:04:39.433 --> 00:04:40.863
cos is close.

00:04:41.485 --> 00:04:43.607
The component close to the angle, the side

00:04:43.667 --> 00:04:45.530
right next to it, uses cos,

00:04:46.111 --> 00:04:47.513
and the other one uses sin.

00:04:48.234 --> 00:04:49.996
It works whichever way the angle is measured,

00:04:50.337 --> 00:04:51.759
as long as you check which side it's

00:04:51.799 --> 00:04:52.120
touching.

00:04:54.692 --> 00:04:55.097
Your turn.

00:04:55.868 --> 00:04:57.710
A ball is launched at ten point zero

00:04:57.751 --> 00:05:00.195
metres per second, at sixty degrees above the

00:05:00.236 --> 00:05:00.736
horizontal.

00:05:01.459 --> 00:05:04.048
Calculate the horizontal and vertical components of its

00:05:04.089 --> 00:05:04.891
initial velocity.

00:05:05.636 --> 00:05:06.483
Pause here to answer.

00:05:17.241 --> 00:05:19.867
The angle is measured from the horizontal, so

00:05:20.148 --> 00:05:22.113
the horizontal side is close to it.

00:05:22.895 --> 00:05:25.943
u x is ten cos sixty, five point

00:05:25.963 --> 00:05:27.728
zero zero metres per second, horizontally.

00:05:28.469 --> 00:05:31.991
u y is ten sin sixty, about eight

00:05:32.031 --> 00:05:33.933
point six six metres per second, upwards.

00:05:34.715 --> 00:05:36.881
It's a steep launch, so more goes up

00:05:37.023 --> 00:05:37.504
than across.

00:05:38.327 --> 00:05:40.594
Swapping them, with eight point six six across,

00:05:40.996 --> 00:05:42.059
is the slip to avoid.

00:05:43.677 --> 00:05:44.162
Last one.

00:05:44.885 --> 00:05:47.507
A projectile is launched with a horizontal velocity

00:05:47.548 --> 00:05:50.189
component of eight point zero metres per second,

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:52.911
and a vertical component of fourteen point seven

00:05:52.952 --> 00:05:54.132
metres per second upwards.

00:05:55.034 --> 00:05:56.895
Calculate the time it takes to reach its

00:05:57.016 --> 00:05:57.676
highest point,

00:05:58.178 --> 00:05:59.378
and state its velocity

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:01.861
and its acceleration at that point.

00:06:02.545 --> 00:06:03.650
Pause here to answer.

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:16.762
At the top, v y is zero, so

00:06:16.982 --> 00:06:19.984
zero equals fourteen point seven minus nine point

00:06:20.084 --> 00:06:20.645
eight t,

00:06:21.225 --> 00:06:23.346
and t is one point five zero seconds.

00:06:24.127 --> 00:06:26.472
The velocity there is eight point zero metres

00:06:26.512 --> 00:06:29.599
per second, horizontally, because the horizontal component never

00:06:29.639 --> 00:06:30.000
changes.

00:06:30.782 --> 00:06:33.509
The acceleration is still nine point eight metres

00:06:33.549 --> 00:06:35.073
per second squared, downwards.

00:06:35.777 --> 00:06:38.609
Saying the velocity or the acceleration is zero

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:40.277
at the top is the classic slip.

00:06:42.228 --> 00:06:44.192
So the launch angle splits the speed,

00:06:44.955 --> 00:06:47.280
u cos theta across and u sin theta

00:06:47.381 --> 00:06:47.481
up.

00:06:48.223 --> 00:06:50.124
The vertical part decides when the ball reaches

00:06:50.165 --> 00:06:52.666
the top, while the horizontal part just carries

00:06:52.767 --> 00:06:52.846
on.

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:55.077
But that's only half the flight.

00:06:55.821 --> 00:06:57.602
How high does the ball get, how long

00:06:57.643 --> 00:06:59.463
is it in the air altogether, and how

00:06:59.545 --> 00:07:00.905
fast is it going when it lands?

00:07:01.654 --> 00:07:02.101
That's next.

00:07:04.828 --> 00:07:05.650
Here are the key notes.

00:07:06.174 --> 00:07:07.721
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:07.762 --> 00:07:08.183
to copy them.
